# Source: Slides "ICT3112 - 7 - Recepy for Loop Invariants - exercises", Exercise 2. The exercise sheet
# "Hoare Logic 2", 3 a), has the same loop, with f = 1 and i = 0 in the precondition instead of the
# initialisation.
id: factorial
title: Factorial
topic: Loop invariants
level: 2
source: Slides 7, Recipe exercises, Exercise 2; exercise sheet "Hoare Logic 2", 3 a
tags: recipe, invariant, factorial, variant

task:
  Find a loop invariant $I$. Prove establishment, preservation and the
  postcondition at the exit. Then give a variant and show that the loop
  terminates. $fact(n)$ is $n!$, with $fact(0) = 1$.

hint:
  Look at the order of the two statements in the body. The variable $i$
  is increased before it is used.

pre: n >= 0
post: f = fact(n)

program:
  {P}
  {R2}
  i := 0;            // i1
  {R1}
  f := 1;            // i2
  {I}
  while (i < n) do {
    {I && i < n}
    {R4}
    i := i + 1;      // b1
    {R3}
    f := f * i       // b2
    {I}
  }
  {I && !(i < n)}
  {Q}

verdict: valid

step: The invariant I
rule: WHILE-CONSEQUENCE
focus: while
text:
  | At the loop head | $i$ | $f$ |
  |---|---|---|
  | before iteration 1 | $0$ | $1$ |
  | before iteration 2 | $1$ | $1$ |
  | before iteration 3 | $2$ | $2$ |
  | before iteration 4 | $3$ | $6$ |

  The trace gives $f = fact(i)$. It is the postcondition $Q$ with $n$
  replaced by the counter $i$. We add the bounds $0 <= i <= n$ of the
  counter, which the exit needs.
set: I = f = fact(i) && 0 <= i <= n
note:
  If you wrote $f = fact(i + 1)$ or $f = fact(i - 1)$, check the first row
  of the trace: $i = 0$ and $f = 1 = fact(0)$.

step: Establishment: R1, the weakest precondition of f := 1
rule: ASSIGN, WP
focus: i2
text:
  The loop is preceded by `i := 0; f := 1`, so establishment is the
  triple
  >>> {«n >= 0 : P»} i := 0; f := 1 {«f = fact(i) && 0 <= i <= n : I»}
  We compute its weakest precondition from the last statement. We
  substitute $f$ with $1$ in its postcondition, the invariant $I$.
calc:
  wp(f := 1, I)
  = (f = fact(i) && 0 <= i <= n)[f <- 1]
  = 1 = fact(i) && 0 <= i <= n       // every f is replaced by 1
set: R1 = 1 = fact(i) && 0 <= i <= n

step: Establishment: R2, the weakest precondition of i := 0
rule: ASSIGN, WP
focus: i1
text:
  We substitute $i$ with $0$ in $R1$, computed in the previous step.
calc:
  wp(i := 0, R1)
  = (1 = fact(i) && 0 <= i <= n)[i <- 0]
  = 1 = fact(0) && 0 <= 0 <= n       // every i is replaced by 0
  = 1 = 1 && 0 <= 0 <= n             // fact(0) = 1
  = 0 <= n                           // 1 = 1 and 0 <= 0 are true
set: R2 = 0 <= n

step: Establishment: the precondition implies R2
rule: CONSEQUENCE
text:
  $R2$ is the weakest precondition of `i := 0; f := 1` for $I$. By the
  consequence rule, establishment holds when the precondition $P$ implies
  $R2$.
vc: n >= 0 ==> 0 <= n
why:
  Both sides say the same thing.

step: Preservation: R3, the weakest precondition of f := f * i
rule: ASSIGN, WP
focus: b2
text:
  Preservation is the triple below, where the loop body is
  `i := i + 1; f := f * i`.
  >>> {«f = fact(i) && 0 <= i <= n : I» && «i < n : B»} i := i + 1; f := f * i {«f = fact(i) && 0 <= i <= n : I»}
  We compute the weakest precondition of the body for $I$, from the last
  statement. We substitute $f$ with $f * i$ in $I$.
calc:
  wp(f := f * i, I)
  = (f = fact(i) && 0 <= i <= n)[f <- f * i]
  = f * i = fact(i) && 0 <= i <= n       // every f is replaced by f * i
set: R3 = f * i = fact(i) && 0 <= i <= n

step: Preservation: R4, the weakest precondition of i := i + 1
rule: ASSIGN, WP
focus: b1
text:
  We substitute $i$ with $i + 1$ in $R3$, computed in the previous step.
calc:
  wp(i := i + 1, R3)
  = (f * i = fact(i) && 0 <= i <= n)[i <- i + 1]
  = f * (i + 1) = fact(i + 1) && 0 <= i + 1 <= n     // every i is replaced by i + 1
set: R4 = f * (i + 1) = fact(i + 1) && 0 <= i + 1 <= n

step: Preservation: I and B imply R4
rule: CONSEQUENCE
text:
  $R4$ is the weakest precondition of the body for $I$. By the consequence
  rule, preservation holds when the invariant $I$ and the loop condition
  $B$ together imply $R4$.
vc: f = fact(i) && 0 <= i <= n && i < n ==> f * (i + 1) = fact(i + 1) && 0 <= i + 1 <= n
why:
  First conjunct: $fact(i + 1) = fact(i) * (i + 1)$, and $f = fact(i)$ by
  $I$, so $f * (i + 1) = fact(i + 1)$.

  Second conjunct: $0 <= i$ gives $0 <= i + 1$, and $i < n$ gives
  $i + 1 <= n$.

step: The postcondition at the exit
rule: CONSEQUENCE, LOGIC
text:
  When the loop stops, $I$ holds and the loop condition $B$ is false. The
  postcondition $Q$ must follow from them.
vc: f = fact(i) && 0 <= i <= n && !(i < n) ==> f = fact(n)
why:
  $!(i < n)$ is $i >= n$. With $i <= n$ from $I$ it gives $i = n$, so
  $f = fact(i)$ becomes $f = fact(n)$.

step: Termination with the variant n - i
rule: WHILE-TOTAL
text:
  The counter $i$ goes up to $n$, so $n - i$ is the number of iterations
  left. We use the variant $E = n - i$. The rule WHILE-TOTAL asks two
  things.

  First, the variant is not negative when an iteration starts.
vc: f = fact(i) && 0 <= i <= n && i < n ==> n - i >= 0
why:
  $i < n$ from $B$ gives $n - i > 0$.
text:
  Second, the body decreases the variant. We write $E0$ for the value of
  $n - i$ before the iteration, and compute the weakest precondition of
  the body for $n - i < E0$, from the last statement.
calc:
  wp(f := f * i, n - i < E0)
  = n - i < E0                // f does not occur, so nothing changes
calc:
  wp(i := i + 1, n - i < E0)
  = n - (i + 1) < E0          // i is replaced by i + 1
vc: f = fact(i) && 0 <= i <= n && i < n && n - i = E0 ==> n - (i + 1) < E0
why:
  $n - (i + 1) = (n - i) - 1 = E0 - 1$, which is smaller than $E0$.
result: The triple is valid, and the loop terminates.
